/**
 * @defgroup poles_calculator Motor Pole Count Calculator
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Classifies an AC motor's pole count from measured speed and line frequency.
 * @details
 * The calculator returns the even pole count used to select the appropriate motor-performance
 * coefficients elsewhere in MEASUR. It accepts a positive measured motor speed and either 50 Hz or
 * 60 Hz line frequency. The result is normally one of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, or 12 poles. This calculation
 * classifies the motor for coefficient selection; it does not measure slip, predict speed, or
 * redesign the motor.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result}
 * The calculation first compares the measured speed with inclusive speed bands for the selected
 * line frequency. Each band corresponds to an even pole count. If no band matches, the calculator
 * applies the fallback described below. The tabulated bands are the primary classification method;
 * the fallback classifies speeds in the gaps between bands.
 *
 * | Pole count | 50 Hz synchronous speed (rpm) | Accepted 50 Hz speed (rpm) | 60 Hz synchronous speed (rpm) | Accepted 60 Hz speed (rpm) |
 * |---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
 * | 2  | 3000 | 2700 to 3000 | 3600 | 3240 to 3600 |
 * | 4  | 1500 | 1350 to 1500 | 1800 | 1620 to 1800 |
 * | 6  | 1000 | 900 to 1000  | 1200 | 1080 to 1200 |
 * | 8  | 750  | 675 to 750   | 900  | 810 to 900 |
 * | 10 | 600  | 540 to 600   | 720  | 648 to 720 |
 * | 12 | 500  | 450 to 500   | 600  | 540 to 600 |
 *
 * Both endpoints are included. For example, a 60 Hz motor measured at either 1620 rpm or 1800 rpm
 * is classified as a four-pole motor.
 *
 * @heading{How to Classify a Motor}
 * 1. Confirm that measured motor speed is positive and choose the nominal 50 Hz or 60 Hz line
 *    frequency.
 * 2. Use the corresponding frequency columns in the table and compare the measured speed with each
 *    inclusive accepted-speed band.
 * 3. Return the pole count for the matching band. These bands are checked before any fallback because
 *    they are the primary MEASUR classification rule.
 * 4. If no band matches, apply the above-range rule or fallback. Treat that result as a
 *    MEASUR-specific classification, especially for a 50 Hz input, rather than a physical estimate.
 * 5. Use the returned pole count only where the downstream motor model requests a pole class, such as
 *    selecting efficiency or full-load-current coefficients.
 *
 * @heading{Synchronous Speed and Pole Count}
 * The rotating magnetic field of an AC motor has synchronous speed:
 * @formula{poles-synchronous-speed;
 *   N_s=\frac{120f}{P}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{N_s; Synchronous speed of the rotating magnetic field; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{f; Electrical line frequency; \hertz}
 * @symrow{P; Total motor pole count; \unitless}
 * @symrow{120; Frequency-to-speed conversion factor; \revolutionsPerMinute\per\hertz}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * This relationship gives the synchronous-speed columns in the table. A 60 Hz four-pole motor, for
 * example, has a synchronous speed of 1800 rpm. The U.S. Department of Energy motor-system
 * sourcebook gives the same relationship and explains that an induction motor operates below
 * synchronous speed because of slip @cite doe2008motorSourcebook.
 *
 * Slip is the fractional difference between synchronous and measured rotor speed:
 * @formula{poles-slip;
 *   s=\frac{N_s-N}{N_s}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{s; Motor slip fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{N_s; Synchronous speed of the rotating magnetic field; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{N; Measured motor rotor speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The calculator's primary bands extend from 90 percent through 100 percent of synchronous speed:
 * @formula{poles-speed-band;
 *   0.9N_s(P,f)\leq N\leq N_s(P,f)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{0.9; Retained lower-band fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{N_s(P,f); Synchronous speed for pole count P and frequency f; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{N; Measured motor rotor speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{P; Candidate even pole count; \unitless}
 * @symrow{f; Electrical line frequency; \hertz}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The 90-percent threshold is an established MEASUR classification boundary, not a statement that
 * normal motor slip is always 10 percent. Actual slip depends on motor design and load. The threshold
 * determines which coefficient tables the motor-efficiency and full-load-current models select.
 *
 * @heading{Fallback Classification}
 * If the measured speed is not in a table band, speeds above 3600 rpm return two poles. All other
 * positive speeds use the historical 60 Hz expression:
 * @formula{poles-fallback;
 *   P_f=\min\left(12,\,2\left\lfloor\frac{\left\lfloor7200/N\right\rfloor}{2}\right\rfloor\right)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_f; Pole count returned by the fallback; \unitless}
 * @symrow{N; Measured motor rotor speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{7200; Numerator derived from 120 times 60 Hz; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{2; Even-pole step size; \unitless}
 * @symrow{12; Maximum returned pole count; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\lfloor\ \rfloor; Floor operation; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The fallback uses the 60 Hz numerator even when the selected line frequency is 50 Hz. It should
 * not be interpreted as a general physical formula for 50 Hz motors. The repository does not
 * record the historical source for this fallback or for the 90-percent band boundary.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example: Primary Speed Band}
 * Consider a 60 Hz motor measured at 1780 rpm. A four-pole motor is the relevant candidate from the
 * table:
 *
 * 1. Its synchronous speed is @math{N_s=(120\times60)/4=1800} rpm.
 * 2. The retained lower boundary is @math{0.9N_s=0.9\times1800=1620} rpm.
 * 3. The measured speed satisfies @math{1620\leq1780\leq1800}, so it is inside the inclusive
 *    four-pole band.
 * 4. The calculator therefore returns four poles. The implied slip is
 *    @math{s=(1800-1780)/1800=0.0111}, or approximately 1.11 percent, which explains why the measured
 *    rotor speed is below synchronous speed.
 *
 * This result tells downstream MEASUR motor models to use their four-pole coefficient data; it does
 * not change the measured speed or report slip as the calculator output.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example: Fallback Classification}
 * Consider a 50 Hz motor measured at 800 rpm. The speed is above the eight-pole band of 675 to 750 rpm
 * and below the six-pole band of 900 to 1000 rpm, so no primary band matches. The retained fallback
 * gives @math{2\left\lfloor\left\lfloor7200/800\right\rfloor/2\right\rfloor=8} poles. The use of
 * 7200 means this result should not be interpreted as a general 50 Hz motor relationship.
 *
 * At either supported frequency, a measured speed of 4000 rpm bypasses the fallback expression and
 * returns two poles through the explicit above-range rule.
 *
 * @heading{Inputs, Limits, and Interpretation}
 * - Motor speed is measured in revolutions per minute and must be positive. Zero or negative speeds
 *   are outside the calculation contract.
 * - Line frequency is selected from the existing `Motor::LineFrequency` values for 50 Hz and 60 Hz.
 * - The result is a discrete pole count, not motor speed or slip.
 * - The calculator is intended to classify conventional AC motor speeds for the coefficient-based
 *   MEASUR motor models. It supports only the nominal 50 Hz and 60 Hz enum values and is not an
 *   arbitrary-frequency variable-frequency-drive pole estimator.
 *
 * @heading{Public Calculation Interface}
 * The C++ API is a namespace function:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * const int pole_count = poles::calculate(1780.0, Motor::LineFrequency::FREQ60);
 * @endcode
 *
 * Poles has no direct WebAssembly function. WebAssembly motor, pump and fan calculations use it
 * internally.
 */
